# mgdecor.msg - the town's furniture (mgdecor.ssl). One entry per line; no braces in comments.
# A prop has two lines: look, examine. A stash has six: look locked, look open, examine, picked, failed, fumbled.

# 100: the notice board inside the gate.
{100}{}{You see the town's notice board.}
{101}{}{MEGATON, in red paint, over the ghost of an airline's name. Below it: TRADERS WELCOME. NO PREACHING AT THE GATE. THE BOMB IS FINE. Somebody has added a question mark after FINE, and somebody else has crossed it out.}

# 110: the sniper's booth outside the gate.
{110}{}{You see a guard booth.}
{111}{}{A toll booth from some pre-War highway, hauled here and promoted to fortification. The sniper's lunch is inside. The sniper, as a rule, is not.}

# 120: the still behind Moriarty's bar.
{120}{}{You see a still.}
{121}{}{Copper pipe, a boiler that used to heat somebody's bath, and a smell that could take the numbers off a vault door. This is where the house whiskey comes from. Now you know, and cannot stop knowing.}

# 130: the shelves of kegs behind a counter (the saloon and the Brass Lantern).
{130}{}{You see a back bar.}
{131}{}{Kegs, jugs and coils. Every vessel has a price chalked on it, and every price has been rubbed out and raised at least once.}

# 140: the Vargas family's home.
{140}{}{You see a wrecked tanker truck.}
{141}{}{A water tanker that died here before the wall went up around it. There are curtains in the cab and a stovepipe through the tank. Somebody calls this home, and keeps it tidier than most houses.}

# 150: the spare deputy in the sheriff's armory.
{150}{}{You see a deactivated robot.}
{151}{}{A second deputy, or the makings of one. A tag wired to its dome reads: STEEL. SPARE. DO NOT SWITCH ON INDOORS (AGAIN).}

# 160: the Confessor's podium.
{160}{}{You see a podium.}
{161}{}{A lectern knocked together from a road sign and a filing cabinet. The top is worn smooth where a fist comes down on it, several times a sermon.}

# 170: the banners of the Children of Atom.
{170}{}{You see a banner of the Children of Atom.}
{171}{}{The old warning sign for radiation, sewn large on red cloth and hung up as a promise.}

# 180: the sign by the water plant.
{180}{}{You see a signboard.}
{181}{}{WATER MERCHANTS. The board came up from somewhere far to the south, on a caravan, a long time ago. Nobody here remembers who they were. The name still fits, so it stays.}

# 190: the sign on the Common House.
{190}{}{You see a sign.}
{191}{}{MOTEL. It lit up once, for most of a second, the day Walter wired it in. The beds inside are free to anyone who has none, which the sign does not say.}

# 200: the outhouses.
{200}{}{You see an outhouse.}
{201}{}{Four planks and a philosophy. The roof went in a dust storm and the town voted, narrowly, to enjoy the view.}

# 210: the tanks outside the water plant.
{210}{}{You see a water tank.}
{211}{}{Riveted steel, beaded with condensation. It ticks as it cools. What comes out of it is the cleanest water for a week's walk, which says more about the walk than the water.}

# 220: the computer in the sheriff's armory.
{220}{}{You see a computer.}
{221}{}{The screen shows two lines, DEPUTY WELD: ACTIVE and DEPUTY STEEL: SEE NOTE, and a prompt for the sheriff's badge number. You do not know it. It does not take suggestions.}

# 230: the warning sign at the pool.
{230}{}{You see a warning sign.}
{231}{}{RADIATION. KEEP OUT. It stands with its back to the pool, facing the congregation, and nobody in town can say whether that was the joke or the mistake.}

# 240: the pumps in the water plant.
{240}{}{You see the water pumps.}
{241}{}{Two pumps, in truth: one that works, and one that is spare parts for the one that works. Both are older than anybody in town. Only one of them complains about it.}

# 250: the sign over the saloon door.
{250}{}{You see a neon sign.}
{251}{}{BAR. Three of its letters still light, which is all of them. Moriarty keeps it burning day and night and puts the cost on everybody's tab.}

# 300: the medicine locker in the clinic.
{300}{}{You see a steel locker with a red cross stenciled on the door. It is locked.}
{301}{}{You see a steel locker with a red cross stenciled on the door.}
{302}{}{The doctor's medicine locker. A note is taped to it: IF YOU CAN READ THIS YOU ARE NOT DYING. WAIT YOUR TURN.}
{303}{}{The lock gives with a small, clinical click.}
{304}{}{The lock stays locked. It was fitted by a man who expects the worst of people and is seldom let down.}
{305}{}{Your pick slips and rattles the whole door. If anyone asks, you were admiring the stencil work.}

# 310: Billy Creel's footlocker.
{310}{}{You see a battered footlocker with a padlock on it.}
{311}{}{You see a battered footlocker. The padlock hangs open.}
{312}{}{Caravan issue, with the stops of a dozen runs burned into the lid with a hot nail. Most of the names are crossed out. One has a small cross next to it instead.}
{313}{}{The padlock drops open.}
{314}{}{The padlock holds. It has seen worse than you between here and the coast.}
{315}{}{The pick jams and you nearly leave it in there. You get it back, less some dignity.}

# 320: the desk in the water plant's back room.
{320}{}{You see a steel desk. The big drawer is locked.}
{321}{}{You see a steel desk. The big drawer stands a finger open.}
{322}{}{A desk for a plant foreman, in a plant that has not had one for years. The dust on top is thick. The dust on the drawer handle is not.}
{323}{}{Something inside slides, and the drawer rolls open.}
{324}{}{The drawer does not budge. Whoever keeps things in it would rather they stayed kept.}
{325}{}{The pick skates across the lock with a screech that sets your teeth on edge. Somewhere behind you a pump skips a beat.}

# 330: the floor safe in the Brass Lantern's kitchen.
{330}{}{You see a floor safe, bolted down and locked.}
{331}{}{You see a floor safe. The door is ajar.}
{332}{}{STAHL is scratched into the door three times, in three different hands. Families are like that.}
{333}{}{The tumblers fall, one, two, three, and the door swings out.}
{334}{}{The dial spins and settles. The safe keeps its own counsel.}
{335}{}{You lose count of the clicks, start again, and lose count again. This round goes to the safe.}

# 400 and up: the custom art that replaced stock props (layout/art.py). Two lines each: look, examine.

# 400: the Confessor's lectern at the pool.
{400}{}{You see a lectern.}
{401}{}{A wheel for a foot, a length of pipe, a locker door for a desk. The open book on it glows faintly, and you would rather not know why. The edge is worn smooth where a fist comes down, several times a sermon.}

# 410: the red banner of the Children of Atom.
{410}{}{You see a banner of the Children of Atom.}
{411}{}{Red cloth on a scrap mast, with three orbits and a nucleus daubed on in white. It is hung where the whole town has to walk past it, as a promise.}

# 420: the yellow banner.
{420}{}{You see a banner of the Children of Atom.}
{421}{}{Yellow once, the color of weak tea now, and torn at the hem. The atom on it has been painted over so many times that it stands out like a scab.}

# 430: the welded atom by the church door.
{430}{}{You see the sign of the Children of Atom.}
{431}{}{An atom welded out of reinforcing bar and set on a pole by the church door. The lump at its heart gives off a small green light, day and night. Nobody will say what it is, and nobody stands next to it for long.}

# 440: the tank in the plant yard.
{440}{}{You see a water tank.}
{441}{}{WATER, stenciled on a boiler shell that came up from the south on a caravan, long ago, with its paperwork. It drips at one seam. In this town a drip is a boast.}

# 450: the warning sign at the pool.
{450}{}{You see a warning sign.}
{451}{}{DANGER, over the three black blades everybody still knows. Somebody has added underneath, smaller: DON'T DRINK IT. It faces the track, not the pool, so the congregation has it at its back.}

# 460: the Brass Lantern's counter.
{460}{}{You see the Brass Lantern's counter.}
{461}{}{A plank bar, a stove that used to be an oil drum, and a wok the size of a hubcap, which is what it was. The soup has been on since before the stools were nailed down. Every bowl has a price chalked on the rim.}

{340}{}{It is not locked.}
{341}{}{You find no traps. Only a lock, doing an honest day's work.}
